Implementation Plan: Custom Shape Cooking Automation

1. System Overview

A high-precision XY(Z) motion system controlled by an ESP32. The user draws shapes in a Flutter app, which converts them to G-code. The G-code is uploaded to the ESP32 via HTTP and executed locally from internal storage (LittleFS) using hardware-timed pulses for maximum smoothness.

2. Hardware Specification

Controller: ESP32 (38-pin DevKit recommended).

Stepper Drivers: A4988 or TMC2209 (Set to 1/16 microstepping).

Axes: * X: Step (GPIO 32), Dir (GPIO 33), Limit (GPIO 16)

Y: Step (GPIO 25), Dir (GPIO 26), Limit (GPIO 17)

Z: Step (GPIO 27), Dir (GPIO 14)

DC Motors: Controlled via MOSFETs on GPIO 12 and 13.

Mechanical Limits: 14,000 steps max travel on X and Y.

3. ESP32 Firmware Development (Arduino/C++)

Core Libraries

FastAccelStepper: For hardware-interrupt-based motion.

GCodeParser: To interpret G0, G1, G4, M3, M5 commands.

LittleFS: To store uploaded G-code recipes locally.

ESPAsyncWebServer: To handle non-blocking file uploads.

Firmware Logic Layers

Homing State Machine: * Sequential homing (X then Y).

Search at 1500 steps/s, backoff 400 steps, slow-touch at 300 steps/s.

G-Code Executor: * Reads .gcode file line-by-line from LittleFS.

Calculates targets for FastAccelStepper.

Synchronizes multi-axis movement using moveTo() and isMoving() checks.

DC Motor Control: * M3 Sxxx: Maps PWM to DC motor pins.

M5: Hard stop for DC motors.

4. Flutter App Development

Drawing & Generation

Canvas Widget: A CustomPainter to record touch points.

Path Processing: * Simplify the path (RDP Algorithm) to reduce unnecessary points.

Scale canvas coordinates (0-100%) to motor steps (0-14,000).

G-Code Logic:

Prepend: G21 (mm) or steps, G90 (Absolute).

Body: Generate G1 X... Y... for each point.

Append: G0 X7000 Y7000 (Return to center), M5 (Stop all).

Communication Layer

File Upload: Send G-code via multipart/form-data to /upload.

Control Commands: * GET /run?file=jalebi.gcode

GET /stop (Emergency Kill)

GET /homing